>  Does everyone have a pet or only someone? Are they allowed to
>have different pets if this is the case? If everyone does, will the pets
>fight among themselves since cat and dog are meant to be natural enemies?

	Sure, let everyone have his/her own pet.  The pets' degree of
  tameness would determine if they fought each other or not.  Besides,
  one *could* find a leash!  (side note..  Wouldn't it be interesting
  to be able to dig a pit or just have a room, for something akin to
  cockfighting?  Players could bet on his/her pet and actually have the
  appropriate gold amount transfer between players).  Also, I'm sure
  some NetHacker's have had more than one pet, via taming, so if you
  did lose your creature you could get another one.  Or maybe you could
  change the loyalty by feeding someone else's pet tripe! (or maybe
  those cut off body parts <smile>...)

> What about levels? I imagine everyone would have to be on the same
>level before they can go down (this would make the system abit easier
>to program) although the other possibility (of everyone being allowed
>to be at different level) is not obviously not out of contention.

	I think that individual players must be allow to journey on
  thier own without waiting for someone else.  I envision some fairly
  capable host machine with different users in different locations. 
  This also leaves the option open for those who want to 'get over' by
  more devious means, such as murder, creating traps (pits), etc.
  And just what will the Gods do if you murder someone?!?  That's 
  something to think about.

> What would you do to win the game? Would the overall objective be to
>just collect the amulet from the wizard (and thus be a group effort)
>or does everyone have to combat some different form of threat to win the game
>(and so everyone can be assessed on individual performances :- in this
>case some can be winner(s) of the game, others losers).

	Have an amulet with the player's name on it.  You all go down, some
  get killed, you get *your* amulet and then you kill your teammate!  
  Wouldn't that just be terrible!?

> Since players can work as teams, why not monsters of the dungeon. 
>In all fairness this should probably also be included. For instance,
>dwarfs combine with goblins to form parties and actually work as parties
>instead of just individuals as they seem to do in normal hack.

	Other creatures already go hacker hunting in packs, let *us* have
  some fun this time!  The Orc runs in a fit of horror!  How about 
  engraving?  "The NetHack SWAT team was here!"  "Hack with the best,
  Die like the rest!" etc etc..  

> Some other interesting points would be that when a character within a
>team dies, his ghost appears and the team would now have to fight him
>now instead of having to wait to next game to do so.

	I think a wand/spell of undead turning can help here.

	Regarding spells, I think they should effect the whole level. If
  someone should use a scroll of magic mapping then everyone should see 
  the fully laid out maze.  If it didn't, what would be the use in banning
  together? Especially if we couldn't use the abilities of the others?
	You are right when you say players would die much easier.  But I
  feel that would add to the fun!  You could bribe a player into giving
  you items.  "I'll use my wand of death on you if you don't give me your
  Dragon Scale Mail!"  But of course you wouldn't care because you're
  wearing a cloak of reflect wand or something... It goes on and on..
  It's really interesting to think about these scenarios.  Keep up the
  discussion!

